   "subject": "Re: problem with coord_map() using large data",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "coord_map splits each segment in small pieces and then projects them.\r<br>This makes it veeeryy slooow with geom_tile because it reprojects\r<br>every piece. It&#39;s not surprising that QGIS is much quicker.\r<br>\r<br>You can try:\r<br>- coord_quickmap which only does an approximate scaling. Should be\r<br>enough for your region of interest\r<br>- geom_raster which creates a raster instead of vector tiles and is\r<br>faster (but writing this I realize that I am not sure it works with\r<br>something else than coord_cartesian).\r<br>\r<br>JiHO\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "\r<br>\r<br>On 7/18/14, <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">gregor...@gmail.com</a> &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">gregor...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:\r<br>&gt; I&#39;m having trouble using coord_map() with a large data set, although it&#39;s\r<br>&gt; working fine with a smaller but otherwise similar data set. With the larger\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; data R simply hangs when trying to create the plot. I&#39;ve pasted the code\r<br>&gt; below and attached a file (large_and_small.R) containing the large and\r<br>&gt; small data sets. Note that the size difference is significant. I would be\r<br>&gt; inclined to write this problem off as one of the projection simply being to\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; computationally difficult with the larger data, but another program (QGIS)\r<br>&gt; can reproject the same data (as a raster layer) almost instantly on the\r<br>&gt; same machine. Any idea why I&#39;m having this problem or how to solve it?\r<br>&gt; Note: Using smaller data might actually be the best solution for me, but\r<br>&gt; that&#39;s a separate question for another post (coming soon).\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; Here&#39;s how I&#39;m creating the plots and more or less what they should look\r<br>&gt; like:\r<br>&gt; load(&quot;large_and_small.R&quot;)\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; library(ggplot2)\r<br>&gt; library(maps)\r<br>&gt; library(RColorBrewer)\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; # Get background map\r<br>&gt; states_map &lt;- map_data(&quot;state&quot;)\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; Here&#39;s what it should look like (except not projected):\r<br>&gt; p &lt;- ggplot(data=states_map) +\r<br>&gt;    geom_polygon(aes(x=long, y=lat, group=group), color=&quot;white&quot;, fill=&quot;dark\r<br>&gt; grey&quot;) +\r<br>&gt; #   coord_map(projection=&quot;mercator&quot;, xlim=c(-125, -100), ylim=c(30, 50)) +\r<br>&gt;    coord_cartesian(xlim=c(-125, -100), ylim=c(30, 50)) +\r<br>&gt;    geom_tile(data=mean_large.df, aes(x=long, y=lat, fill=mean)) +\r<br>&gt;    scale_fill_gradientn(colours=brewer.pal(7,&quot;YlOrRd&quot;))\r<br>&gt; p\r<br>&gt;\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "&gt; &lt;<a href=\"https://lh4.googleusercontent.com/-b1p62Zq23gk/U8lgMGSWXII/AAAAAAAAAQI/ag-CxRGgOcg/s1600/mean_large_unprojected.jpeg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://lh4.googleusercontent.com/-b1p62Zq23gk/U8lgMGSWXII/AAAAAAAAAQI/ag-CxRGgOcg/s1600/mean_large_unprojected.jpeg&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792370478080000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0Qw_rqUrBaVtbPkh39Jyao\">https://lh4.googleusercontent.com/-b1p62Zq23gk/U8lgMGSWXII/AAAAAAAAAQI/ag-CxRGgOcg/s1600/mean_large_unprojected.jpeg</a>&gt;\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "&gt;\r<br>&gt; Here&#39;s what the smaller data look like in a projected plot.\r<br>&gt; p &lt;- ggplot(data=states_map) +\r<br>&gt;    geom_polygon(aes(x=long, y=lat, group=group), color=&quot;white&quot;, fill=&quot;dark\r<br>&gt; grey&quot;) +\r<br>&gt;    coord_map(projection=&quot;mercator&quot;, xlim=c(-125, -100), ylim=c(30, 50)) +\r<br>&gt; #   coord_cartesian(xlim=c(-125, -100), ylim=c(30, 50)) +\r<br>&gt;    geom_tile(data=mean_small.df, aes(x=long, y=lat, fill=mean)) +\r<br>&gt;    scale_fill_gradientn(colours=brewer.pal(7,&quot;YlOrRd&quot;))\r<br>&gt; p\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;\r<br>"
    ],
    [
